menu "app_ota -- firmware updates"

config APP_OTA_ENABLE
    bool "Enable OTA updates"
    default y

config APP_OTA_BASE_URL
    string "Base URL of the firmware server"
    default "https://nas.lan:8443/fw"
    depends on APP_OTA_ENABLE
    help
        The device fetches <base>/<project>/<chip>/manifest.json, which is exactly the
        layout tools/publish.sh writes. Keep this in step with OTA_BASE_URL when you run
        that script, or devices will look somewhere nothing was published.

        Use whichever of the hostname or the IP appears in the server certificate's SAN
        list -- a mismatch surfaces as a TLS handshake failure that looks like a network
        fault. An IP avoids depending on the LAN's DNS; a name survives the NAS moving.

config APP_OTA_POLL_ENABLE
    bool "Poll for updates periodically"
    default y
    depends on APP_OTA_ENABLE

config APP_OTA_POLL_INTERVAL_S
    int "Seconds between update checks"
    default 21600
    range 60 604800
    depends on APP_OTA_POLL_ENABLE
    help
        Six hours by default. The interval is jittered so a fleet that all booted after
        the same power cut does not hit the server in the same second.

config APP_OTA_POLL_JITTER_PCT
    int "Jitter applied to the poll interval, percent"
    default 20
    range 0 50
    depends on APP_OTA_POLL_ENABLE

config APP_OTA_HTTP_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "HTTP timeout, milliseconds"
    default 15000
    depends on APP_OTA_ENABLE
    help
        Applies per socket operation, not to the whole download -- a 900 KB image over
        poor Wi-Fi legitimately takes minutes.

config APP_OTA_REQUIRE_TIME_SYNC
    bool "Refuse to start an update before the clock is set"
    default y
    depends on APP_OTA_ENABLE
    help
        Certificate validity cannot be checked on a device that thinks it is 1970, so
        without this the first TLS handshake fails in a way that looks like a network
        problem (PLAN §7). Turning it off is only sensible on a network with no route to
        any time source, and then you are trusting an unvalidated clock.

config APP_OTA_TIME_SYNC_WAIT_S
    int "Seconds to wait for the clock before giving up on a check"
    default 45
    range 0 300
    depends on APP_OTA_REQUIRE_TIME_SYNC
    help
        SNTP timing varies with whether the DHCP-supplied server answers -- 7 s and 90 s
        have both been observed on the same network. Waiting turns a check triggered just
        after boot from a spurious failure into a short delay.

config APP_OTA_RESTART_DELAY_MS
    int "Delay before restarting into a new image, milliseconds"
    default 2000
    depends on APP_OTA_ENABLE
    help
        Long enough for the web UI to poll /api/status once more and show that the
        update finished, rather than the page simply dying mid-request.

config APP_OTA_TASK_STACK
    int "OTA task stack size"
    default 8192
    depends on APP_OTA_ENABLE
    help
        TLS needs room. Below about 6 KB the handshake overflows this stack rather than
        failing cleanly.

config APP_OTA_REQUIRE_SHA256
    bool "Refuse an image whose sha256 the manifest did not publish"
    default y
    depends on APP_OTA_ENABLE
    help
        The written slot is hashed and compared with the manifest before the slot is
        marked bootable. tools/publish.sh always writes the field, so a manifest without
        one is either hand-edited or half-written -- and a half-written manifest next to
        a half-uploaded image is precisely the case worth refusing.

        Turning this off downgrades a missing hash to a warning. A hash that is present
        and wrong is always fatal, either way.

config APP_OTA_MAX_FAILURES
    int "Attempts before a version is left alone"
    default 2
    range 0 10
    depends on APP_OTA_ENABLE
    help
        A version that has been installed this many times and never survived its
        self-test is skipped by the periodic poll. Without it, one bad build published to
        the NAS means every device downloads 900 KB, reboots, rolls back and does it
        again on every poll, for as long as it stays published.

        Pressing "Check for updates" retries it anyway -- the quarantine is there to stop
        automation looping, not to stop a human. 0 disables it entirely.

menu "Self-test -- what a new image must prove"
    depends on APP_OTA_ENABLE && BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE

comment "Rollback is off; a new image is never put on probation"
    depends on !BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE

config APP_OTA_SELFTEST_MIN_UPTIME_S
    int "Seconds the image must run before it can be confirmed"
    default 120
    range 0 3600
    help
        Confirming an image cancels the rollback, so it should not happen in the first
        second of the first boot. Long enough to catch a crash during start-up, short
        enough that a device is not left one reset away from reverting a good image.

config APP_OTA_SELFTEST_REQUIRE_SERVER
    bool "Require the update server to be reachable"
    default y
    help
        Fetches the manifest over TLS as the last step of the self-test. This is the
        check that matters: it proves the new image can still be *updated*, which is the
        only property that guarantees a fix can reach the device later.

        Note what it implies. An image that boots perfectly but has broken Wi-Fi, DNS,
        the clock or the embedded CA fails the self-test and is rolled back -- correctly,
        because a device that cannot be updated is a device that needs a cable. The cost
        is that a good image also rolls back on a device whose *network* is genuinely
        gone, and it will be offered again once the network is back.

config APP_OTA_SELFTEST_TIMEOUT_S
    int "Seconds to reach all of the above before giving up"
    default 600
    range 60 7200
    help
        Ten minutes. Generous on purpose: SNTP alone has been seen to take 90 s, and a
        device that boots before its access point does should be given time to associate
        rather than reverting a perfectly good image.

config APP_OTA_SELFTEST_RETRY_S
    int "Seconds between server probes during the self-test"
    default 30
    range 5 600

endmenu

endmenu
